    <h2>1. Recovery Agent Conduct & Calling Hours</h2>

    <h3>Can recovery agents call me at 9:00 PM or on Sundays?</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>No.</strong> Under the <strong>RBI Master Direction on Fair Practices Code for Lenders</strong> and the Outsourcing of Financial Services circular, recovery agents are strictly prohibited from contacting borrowers before <strong>8:00 AM and after 7:00 PM</strong> on any day. This restriction applies across phone calls, automated IVR calls, SMS, WhatsApp messages, and physical visits. Contacting a borrower outside this window is an actionable regulatory violation.
    </p>

    <h3>Can collection agents visit my workplace or tell my boss about my personal loan?</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>No.</strong> Under RBI directives, collection agents are barred from visiting a borrower's workplace without prior written consent and appointment. Furthermore, disclosing a borrower's debt status to employers, colleagues, friends, or non-borrower family members is an illegal breach of privacy and a direct violation of RBI guidelines.
    </p>

    <h3>Can a recovery agent threaten me with immediate police arrest for defaulting on a personal loan?</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>No.</strong> Default on an unsecured personal loan or credit card in India is fundamentally a civil breach of contract, not a criminal offence. The police do not possess statutory jurisdiction to arrest citizens for inability to pay an unsecured bank loan. Such threats constitute criminal intimidation under the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS) and must be reported immediately to the bank's Principal Nodal Officer and local police.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Credit Bureau (CIBIL) Disputes & Compensation</h2>

    <h3>How long do banks and CIBIL have to fix an error on my credit report?</h3>
    <p>
      Under <strong>RBI Circular RBI/2023-24/72</strong>, the entire dispute resolution process must be completed within <strong>30 calendar days</strong> from the date you lodge the dispute. The originating lending bank is given up to 21 calendar days to verify its records, and the credit bureau (CIBIL, Experian, Equifax, CRIF) is given the remaining 9 calendar days to update your file.
    </p>

    <h3>What happens if the bank takes more than 30 days to resolve my credit report dispute?</h3>
    <p>
      Under circular RBI/2023-24/72, if the dispute remains unresolved past 30 calendar days, the defaulting institution (bank or credit bureau) is legally required to pay you statutory compensation of <strong>₹100 per calendar day of delay</strong>. The compensation must be credited directly to your bank account within 5 working days of resolution.
    </p>

    <h3>Will settling a loan (OTS) remove the negative mark from CIBIL?</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>No.</strong> When you negotiate a One-Time Settlement (OTS) with a haircut, the bank reports the account status as <strong>"Settled"</strong>, not "Closed". Under the Credit Information Companies (Regulation) Act, this "Settled" status remains on your credit file for <strong>7 years</strong>, signaling to future lenders that you did not pay the full contractual debt. To convert a "Settled" tag to "Closed", you must pay the original waived haircut balance to the bank and obtain a clean No Objection Certificate (NOC).
    </p>

    <h2>3. Secured Loans & SARFAESI Procedures</h2>

    <h3>Can a bank seize my house immediately after I miss 2 home loan EMIs?</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>No.</strong> The SARFAESI Act, 2002 prescribes strict milestones. First, the account must be overdue for more than 90 days to be classified as a Non-Performing Asset (NPA). Next, the bank must issue a formal demand notice under Section 13(2) giving you <strong>60 full calendar days</strong> to clear the dues. Furthermore, if you file an objection under Section 13(3A), the bank must reply within 15 days. Only after these periods expire can the bank attempt symbolic possession under Section 13(4).
    </p>

    <h3>Where can I challenge a bank's SARFAESI possession notice? Can I go to civil court?</h3>
    <p>
      Under Section 34 of the SARFAESI Act, the jurisdiction of regular civil courts is completely barred. You must file a <strong>Securitisation Application (SA) before the Debt Recovery Tribunal (DRT)</strong> under Section 17 within <strong>45 calendar days</strong> from the date of the possession measure.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Cheque Bounces & eNACH Mandates</h2>

    <h3>Does an eNACH or ECS auto-debit bounce carry the same criminal penalty as a paper cheque bounce?</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Yes.</strong> While physical cheques are governed by Section 138 of the Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881, electronic auto-debit bounces (eNACH, NACH, ECS) are governed by <strong>Section 25 of the Payment and Settlement Systems Act, 2007 (PSSA)</strong>. Section 25 provides identical criminal penalties: imprisonment up to 2 years, or a monetary fine up to twice the dishonoured amount, or both. However, the lender must still issue a formal written legal notice within 30 days giving you 15 days to pay before a criminal complaint can be filed.
    </p>
